// Brought to u by MidManStudio
//
// AS2: the async core. This template edits existing docs and site pages. The code, the tests and the
// fixtures come in the tarball as2-async-core-replacements.tar.gz.
// Anchored edits (each anchor occurs exactly once) and one block replacement that keeps its anchor lines:
//   docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md              AS2 decisions, section 1 brought up to date, 2.1, section 5 items 2, 4 and 5, AS2 done
//   docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md              TIER-002 and TIER-003 retired, TYPE-143 and TYPE-144 added, PARSE-004 text
//   docs/PARSER_RULES.md                   section 5.7c, two rows of the belongs-where table
//   docs/ubel.ebnf                         the async result forms, AsyncBlock, the tier summary
//   web/site/src/reference/grammar.md      the same grammar edits (the page is the grammar verbatim)
//   docs/MEMORY_MODEL.md, TRAITS_TIERS_SPEC.md, ubel_stratum_rd.md, NAMING_CONVENTIONS.md, PARKED_IDEAS.md   one statement each
//   web/site/src/tier-model.md             the await column
//   web/site/src/errors/index.md, tier.md, parse.md, type.md   TIER-002 and TIER-003 out, PARSE-004 example, TYPE-143 and TYPE-144 with verified examples
//   web/site/src/guide/async.md            status and examples that run; guide/errors-and-optionals.md   the Async paragraph
//   web/site/src/project-status.md         what landed and the gaps
//   crates/ubel_llvm/src/emit.rs            three lines: an async block is reported unsupported (an anchored edit, because the LLVM backend moves that file)
// This overwrites the earlier patch.mdix, which is right: there is only ever one.
// Checked on a fresh clone of live with the real applier.

@CONFIG(
  version    -> "1.0.0",
  author     -> "MidManStudio",
  features   -> "quickfuncs,data",
  debug_mode -> "off"
)

@QUICKFUNCS(
  ~patch_replace_text<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "replace_text", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }

  ~patch_replace_block_keep<object>(_path, _start, _end, _content) {
    return {
      path         = _path
      op           = "replace_block"
      start_anchor = _start
      end_anchor   = _end
      content      = _content
      keep_anchors = "true"
    }
  }
)

@DATA(
  patch_files::
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "## 1. Where things stand (probed 2026-11-14 on live master)", "### AS2 decisions (2026-11-17)\n\n- An async function is written in the C# form, `async fn f() Task<T>`, with a body that returns\n  `T`. A task is awaited at most once. The cleanup trait that `using` calls is `Dispose`. These are\n  items 2 (signature part), 4 and 5 of section 5, as recommended.\n- With no result, an async function is written bare: `async fn run()` means `Task<void>`\n  (**against the recommendation**, which was an explicit `Task<void>` and an error for a bare\n  `async fn`). Cost: two spellings of one type are accepted, and only functions with a result show\n  the task in their signature.\n- `await` outside an async function or block is an error in every tier, as recommended.\n- `async { ... }` blocks are built in AS2 (**against the recommendation**, which was an explicit\n  error until a later slice). Cost: a flag on the lambda node that every pass has to respect,\n  closure capture rules applied to tasks, and the sendable rule at AS4 has to cover them.\n- Settled without a question: methods in an `impl` or `extend` block may be async; `async` on a\n  trait method or on a method of a trait impl is `PARSE-004` until AS7; a task that is never\n  awaited does nothing and is not reported; `async fn main` is allowed and run to completion,\n  because with `await` limited to async code and no spawn before AS3, nothing else could start an\n  async function from a synchronous `main`.\n\n## 1. Where things stand (probed 2026-11-14 on live master; async updated when AS2 landed, 2026-11-17)")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "- **No program can produce a `Task`.** A call to `async fn f() int` has type `int`, so `await` on\n  it is `TYPE-106`. `async fn f() Task<int> { return x * 2 }` is `TYPE-101`, because the body\n  must itself return a `Task`. `async { 5 }` parses as an ordinary block, the same as `unsafe`.\n  `Task` is a keyword token, so `Task.new(5)` is a parse error. `await` type-checks only on a\n  parameter declared `Task<T>`, and nothing can supply one.\n- `await` outside HIGH is `TIER-002`. `async fn` in MID or LOW is `PARSE-004`.\n- The interpreter treats `await` as a no-op (`crates/core/src/interpreter/eval/expr.rs`) and has\n  no task value. No fixture mentions `async` or `await`.\n- An `async fn` example written in the earlier public language tour (declared `Task<User>!`, body\n  returning the user) has the same shape as the failing `Task<int>` probe, so it did not run. The\n  rewritten tour has no async section, and the site's Async chapter is still a placeholder.", "- **A task can be built (AS2, 2026-11-17).** `async fn f() Task<int>` has the call type\n  `Task<int>` and a body that returns `int`; a bare `async fn f()` is `Task<void>`; `async { }` is a\n  `Task` over a block. A declared result that is not a task is `TYPE-144`. A call or a block builds\n  a cold task and runs nothing.\n- `async fn` and `await` are legal in every tier. `await` outside an async function or block is\n  `TYPE-143`, and a plain lambda inside an async function is not an async context. `TIER-002`,\n  `TIER-003` and the `PARSE-004` for `async fn` outside HIGH are retired. `async` on a trait method,\n  or on a method of a trait impl, is `PARSE-004` until AS7.\n- The interpreter has a task value (`Value::Task`). `await` runs the task where it is awaited and\n  marks it awaited, and a second `await` of the same task is a run-time error. The move checker\n  tracks only LOW-tier `Unique` locals, so there is no compile-time error for a second `await` yet.\n  `main` may be async and is run to completion. The native backend reports async functions, `await`\n  and async blocks as unsupported.\n- The earlier public tour wrote an `async fn` declared `Task<User>!` with a body returning the user;\n  that example did not run when it was written. The Async guide now has examples that do.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "  across a wait (A8). How the return type is written in the signature is an open detail\n  (section 5, item 2).", "  across a wait (A8). An async function is written `async fn f() Task<T>` and its body returns\n  `T`; with no result it is written bare, `async fn run()`, and means `Task<void>`. `async { ... }`\n  is a cold task over a block that captures its scope as a closure does.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "- `await task` runs the task to completion from the awaiting task and yields its result. Awaiting\n  a cold task inline allocates nothing; the machine runs inside the awaiting task's frame.", "- `await task` runs the task to completion from the awaiting task and yields its result. Awaiting\n  a cold task inline allocates nothing; the machine runs inside the awaiting task's frame.\n- `await` is legal only directly inside an async function or an async block. `main` may be async;\n  the interpreter runs it to completion, because nothing else can start async code before\n  `Task.spawn` exists (AS3).")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "- `TIER-002` and the parse error for `async fn` in MID or LOW are retired. The `await` column of\n  the tier table in `tier-model.md`, the Async chapter of the site when it is written,\n  `MEMORY_MODEL.md` section 8 and `PARSER_RULES.md` section 11 all change when this is built.", "- `TIER-002`, `TIER-003` and the parse error for `async fn` in MID or LOW are retired (done in AS2),\n  and the `await` column of the tier table in `tier-model.md`, the Async guide, `MEMORY_MODEL.md`\n  section 8 and `PARSER_RULES.md` section 11 say so.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "2. **Signature and typing of async functions and failed tasks.**\n   (a) The declared return type. Either `async fn f() T`, where a call has type `Task<T>`, or\n   `async fn f() Task<T>` with a body that returns `T` (the C# form, which the earlier public\n   tour wrote). Recommended: the C# form, so `Task<T>` stays visible in the signature and the\n   form already shown in public documentation stays valid.\n   (b) Failure. Candidates: `await` yields `T` and re-raises a captured panic in the awaiting task,\n   with a separate `join` returning `T!`; or `await` always yields a failure value. Also the\n   placement of `!` for expected errors: `Task<User!>` against `Task<User>!` (the earlier tour wrote the\n   second form, which then means the call itself can fail). Recommended: `Task<User!>`, with\n   `await` re-raising a captured panic and `Task.join` returning the error.", "2. **Signature and typing of async functions and failed tasks.** *Signature decided 2026-11-17:*\n   `async fn f() Task<T>` with a body that returns `T`, and a bare `async fn run()` for no result,\n   which means `Task<void>` (against the recommendation of an explicit `Task<void>`). *Failure\n   typing is still open and belongs to AS6:* the placement of `!` for expected errors, `Task<User!>`\n   against `Task<User>!` (the earlier tour wrote the second form, which then means the call itself\n   can fail). Recommended: `Task<User!>`, with `await` re-raising a captured panic and `Task.join`\n   returning the error. AS2 already accepts `Task<User!>` as a declared result and rejects\n   `Task<User>!` (`TYPE-144`).")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "4. **Awaiting a task more than once.** Recommended: a task is awaited at most once. A second\n   await is a compile error where the move checker sees it and a run-time error in HIGH.\n5. **Cleanup trait name.** `using` calls a cleanup method at the closing brace. Candidates:\n   `Drop`, `Dispose`. Recommended: `Dispose`, matching `using`.", "4. **Awaiting a task more than once.** *Decided 2026-11-17:* a task is awaited at most once. A second\n   await is a run-time error in every tier. The compile-time error where the move checker can see\n   it is not built: the move checker tracks only LOW-tier `Unique` locals, so it needs a pass over\n   task values first.\n5. **Cleanup trait name.** *Decided 2026-11-17:* `Dispose`, matching `using`.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md", "| **AS2** | Async core in sema and the interpreter:", "| **AS2** | **Done 2026-11-17.** Async core in sema and the interpreter:")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "| TIER-002 | AwaitInWrongTier |", "| TIER-002 | *(retired; was `AwaitInWrongTier`. `await` is legal in every tier since AS2, see the detailed entry below)* |")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "| TIER-003 | AsyncFunctionNotHigh |", "| TIER-003 | *(retired; was `AsyncFunctionNotHigh`. `async fn` is legal in every tier since AS2, see the detailed entry below)* |")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "| TYPE-142 | NotAStruct |", "| TYPE-142 | NotAStruct |\n| TYPE-143 | AwaitOutsideAsync |\n| TYPE-144 | AsyncReturnNotTask |")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "- `TIER-002` `AwaitInWrongTier` — *Error*. \"`await` is only valid in `@tier(high)`; this function is `@tier(x)`\". Suggestion: annotate with `@tier(high)` or remove the `await`.\n- `TIER-003` `AsyncFunctionNotHigh` — *Error*. \"async functions must be `@tier(high)`; this function is `@tier(x)`\". Suggestion: add `@tier(high)` or remove `async`.", "- `TIER-002` — *Retired.* Was `AwaitInWrongTier`: \"`await` is only valid in `@tier(high)`; this function is `@tier(x)`\". `await` and `async fn` are legal in every tier since AS2 (2026-11-17). What an `await` needs now is an async context, which is `TYPE-143`. The number is not reused.\n- `TIER-003` — *Retired.* Was `AsyncFunctionNotHigh`: \"async functions must be `@tier(high)`; this function is `@tier(x)`\". An `async fn` is legal in every tier since AS2. `async` on a trait method stays an error until AS7, as `PARSE-004`. The number is not reused.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "name of a struct; write a value of `int` directly, or alias a struct\".", "name of a struct; write a value of `int` directly, or alias a struct\".\n- `TYPE-143` `AwaitOutsideAsync` — *Error*. \"`await` is only valid inside an async function or an async block\". An `await` in a plain function, in a method that is not async, in a constant or static initializer, or inside a plain lambda (even one written in an async function: `await` does not reach through a closure). Raised by type inference from a flag it keeps for the body it is checking; one report per `await`. No suggestion.\n- `TYPE-144` `AsyncReturnNotTask` — *Error*. \"an async function returns `Task<T>`, found `int`\". An `async fn` whose declared result is not a `Task<T>`, written at the function (`async fn f() int`, `async fn f() void`, `Task<int>!`). The declared type is `Task<T>` and the body returns `T`; with no declared result, `async fn f()` is `Task<void>` and is fine. `Task<int!>`, a task that yields a fallible value, is fine. One report per function, at the signature. No suggestion.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "around a const or static is not an own annotation and is not reported.", "around a const or static is not an own annotation and is not reported. `async` on a method declared in a trait, or on a method of a trait impl, is this error too (\"async methods in a trait are not supported yet\", until async trait methods are built). `async fn` outside `@tier(high)` was reported here until AS2 and is legal now.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "same shape as `AwaitInWrongTier` but keyed", "same shape as the retired `AwaitInWrongTier` but keyed")
    patch_replace_text("docs/DIAGNOSTICS_RULES.md", "(`AwaitInWrongTier`, `IllegalTierCall`, ...)", "(`MethodInWrongTier`, `IllegalTierCall`, ...)")
    patch_replace_text("docs/PARSER_RULES.md", "### 5.8 `{` after a bare identifier — Struct Literal vs Block", "### 5.7c `async` and `await`\n\n`async fn` and `await` are parsed in every tier: the parser holds no tier rule for either. An async\nfunction or method records `is_async` and nothing else. `async { ... }` is not an expression of its\nown: it parses to a `Lambda` with no parameters and `is_async_block` set, so every pass that handles\na closure handles the block, and only the passes that care about the difference read the flag (type\ninference gives it the type `Task<T>`, the interpreter builds a cold task, and the native emitter\nreports it unsupported).\n\nThe parser rejects one thing here: `async` on a method declared in a trait, or on a method of a\ntrait impl, is `PARSE-004` (\"async methods in a trait are not supported yet\") until async trait\nmethods are built. Whether an `await` sits inside an async function or block (`TYPE-143`) and\nwhether an async function declares a `Task<T>` (`TYPE-144`) are type inference's rules.\n\n### 5.8 `{` after a bare identifier — Struct Literal vs Block")
    patch_replace_text("docs/PARSER_RULES.md", "| Does `await` appear outside HIGH tier? | **Parser**: warn; **TierChecker**: hard error (today; the rule is being opened to every tier, see `docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md`) |", "| Is this `await` inside an async function or block? | **TypeInfer** (`TYPE-143`); the parser accepts `await` anywhere |\n| Does an async function declare a `Task<T>`? | **TypeInfer** (`TYPE-144`) |")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel.ebnf", "ReturnSpec    ::= TypeExpr \"!\"?\n                | \"Task\" \"<\" TypeExpr \">\" \"!\"?\n", "ReturnSpec    ::= TypeExpr \"!\"?\n                | \"Task\" \"<\" TypeExpr \">\" \"!\"?\n\n(* An async function declares the task it returns: `async fn f() Task<int>`, with a body *)\n(* that returns an int. With no ReturnSpec, `async fn f()` means Task<void>. `async` on a   *)\n(* trait method, or on a method of a trait impl, is not accepted yet.                      *)\n")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/reference/grammar.md", "ReturnSpec    ::= TypeExpr \"!\"?\n                | \"Task\" \"<\" TypeExpr \">\" \"!\"?\n", "ReturnSpec    ::= TypeExpr \"!\"?\n                | \"Task\" \"<\" TypeExpr \">\" \"!\"?\n\n(* An async function declares the task it returns: `async fn f() Task<int>`, with a body *)\n(* that returns an int. With no ReturnSpec, `async fn f()` means Task<void>. `async` on a   *)\n(* trait method, or on a method of a trait impl, is not accepted yet.                      *)\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel.ebnf", "                | LambdaExpr\n                | IfExpr\n", "                | LambdaExpr\n                | AsyncBlock\n                | IfExpr\n")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/reference/grammar.md", "                | LambdaExpr\n                | IfExpr\n", "                | LambdaExpr\n                | AsyncBlock\n                | IfExpr\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel.ebnf", "PrimaryExpr   ::= Literal\n", "(* `async { ... }` is a cold task over a block. It parses to a lambda with no parameters,  *)\n(* so it captures its scope as a closure does; `return` ends the block and `await` is    *)\n(* legal inside it.                                                                       *)\nAsyncBlock    ::= \"async\" Block\n\nPrimaryExpr   ::= Literal\n")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/reference/grammar.md", "PrimaryExpr   ::= Literal\n", "(* `async { ... }` is a cold task over a block. It parses to a lambda with no parameters,  *)\n(* so it captures its scope as a closure does; `return` ends the block and `await` is    *)\n(* legal inside it.                                                                       *)\nAsyncBlock    ::= \"async\" Block\n\nPrimaryExpr   ::= Literal\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel.ebnf", "(*   No async/await                                             *)\n", "(*   async/await allowed                                        *)\n")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/reference/grammar.md", "(*   No async/await                                             *)\n", "(*   async/await allowed                                        *)\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel.ebnf", "(*   No async/await, no GC, no arena                           *)\n", "(*   async/await allowed; no GC, no arena                       *)\n")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/reference/grammar.md", "(*   No async/await, no GC, no arena                           *)\n", "(*   async/await allowed; no GC, no arena                       *)\n")
    patch_replace_text("docs/TRAITS_TIERS_SPEC.md", "An `async` method that\n   inherits `LOW` or `MID` is now `TIER-003`, the same rule as an `async fn` in\n   a tier block; writing `@tier(high)` on it keeps it legal.", "An `async` method is\n   legal in every tier (`TIER-003` was retired in AS2); `async` on a trait method\n   stays an error (`PARSE-004`) until async trait methods are built.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/ubel_stratum_rd.md", "an `async` method that inherits `LOW` is `TIER-003`, one that writes `@tier(high)`\n  is fine.", "an `async` method is legal in every tier (one that inherited `LOW` was `TIER-003`\n  until AS2).")
    patch_replace_text("docs/NAMING_CONVENTIONS.md", "used for HIGH-tier async return types", "used for async return types")
    patch_replace_text("docs/PARKED_IDEAS.md", "(`check_callee_tier`, the same machinery behind the\n  `await`-only-in-HIGH-tier rule) as its core job today.", "(`check_callee_tier`, the machinery behind the cross-tier call rule) as its core job today.")
    patch_replace_text("docs/MEMORY_MODEL.md", "✅ **Existing precedent:** `await` (`ExprKind::Await`) is already\nhard-gated to `@tier(high)` functions only, at the expression level. Decided\n2026-11-14 and not yet built: `await` and `async fn` open to every tier (`docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md`).", "✅ **Former precedent:** `await` (`ExprKind::Await`) was hard-gated to `@tier(high)` functions\nat the expression level. Since AS2 (2026-11-17) `await` and `async fn` are legal in every tier\n(`docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md`), and an `await` needs an async context instead (`TYPE-143`).")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/tier-model.md", "| Arena allocated    | Not allowed |", "| Arena allocated    | Allowed |")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/tier-model.md", "| Manual, borrow-checked | Not allowed |", "| Manual, borrow-checked | Allowed |")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/errors/index.md", "| [TIER-002](./tier.md#tier-002) | AwaitInWrongTier \\* |\n| [TIER-003](./tier.md#tier-003) | AsyncFunctionNotHigh \\* |\n", "")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/errors/index.md", "`TIER-007` is retired and its number is not reused.", "`TIER-002`, `TIER-003` and `TIER-007` are retired and their numbers are not reused.")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/errors/index.md", "| [TYPE-142](./type.md#type-142) | NotAStruct |", "| [TYPE-142](./type.md#type-142) | NotAStruct |\n| [TYPE-143](./type.md#type-143) | AwaitOutsideAsync |\n| [TYPE-144](./type.md#type-144) | AsyncReturnNotTask |")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/errors/tier.md", "### TIER-002\n\n**AwaitInWrongTier** — `` `await` is only valid in `@tier(high)`; this function is `@tier(x)` ``\n\n`await` is used outside HIGH tier.\n\n*No verified example yet.*\n\n**Fix:** Annotate the function `@tier(high)`, or remove the `await`.\n\n### TIER-003\n\n**AsyncFunctionNotHigh** — `` async functions must be `@tier(high)`; this function is `@tier(x)` ``\n\nAn async function declared outside HIGH tier. In the compiler as it stands, `async fn` under `@tier(mid)` is already reported by the parser as [PARSE-004](./parse.md#parse-004), so that is what you will see for that case.\n\n*No verified example yet.*\n\n**Fix:** Add `@tier(high)`, or remove `async`.\n\n", "")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/errors/parse.md", "and `async fn` outside `@tier(high)`. A `@tier` block", "and `async` on a method declared in a trait or in a trait impl. A `@tier` block")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/errors/parse.md", "<!-- ubel-test: error PARSE-004 -->\n```ubel\n@tier(mid)\nasync fn run() {\n}\n\nfn main() {\n}\n```\n\n```text\n>>> error[PARSE-004]: `async fn` is not allowed here: async functions are only permitted in @tier(high) context\n  --> 2:1\n    |\n  2 | async fn run() {\n    | ^^^^^\n  ~> help: remove @tier(mid) / @tier(low), or remove 'async'\n  <~\n<<<\n```", "<!-- ubel-test: error PARSE-004 -->\n```ubel\ntrait Job {\n    async fn go(self) Task<int>\n}\n\nfn main() {\n}\n```\n\n```text\n>>> error[PARSE-004]: `async fn` is not allowed here: async methods in a trait are not supported yet\n  --> 2:5\n    |\n  2 |     async fn go(self) Task<int>\n    |     ^^^^^\n  ~> help: write a plain method that returns a `Task<T>` from a free async function, or wait for async trait methods\n  <~\n<<<\n```")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/errors/type.md", "**Fix:** Use an operator that fits the type; for booleans use `and` and `or`.\n", "**Fix:** Use an operator that fits the type; for booleans use `and` and `or`.\n\n### TYPE-143\n\n**AwaitOutsideAsync** — `` `await` is only valid inside an async function or an async block ``\n\nAn `await` that is not directly inside an async function or an async block: in a plain function, in a method that is not async, in a constant or static initializer, or inside a plain lambda. A lambda inside an async function is not an async context, because `await` does not reach through a closure. It is reported in every tier.\n\n<!-- ubel-test: error TYPE-143 -->\n```ubel\nasync fn one() Task<int> {\n    return 1\n}\n\nfn plain() int {\n    return await one()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n}\n```\n\n```text\n>>> error[TYPE-143]: `await` is only valid inside an async function or an async block\n  --> 6:12\n    |\n  6 |     return await one()\n    |            ^^^^^^^^^^^\n<<<\n```\n\n**Fix:** Make the enclosing function `async` and declare the task it returns, or build the work as an `async { ... }` block and await that from async code.\n\n### TYPE-144\n\n**AsyncReturnNotTask** — `` an async function returns `Task<T>`, found `int` ``\n\nAn `async fn` whose declared result is not a `Task<T>`. The declared type is the task and the body returns the `T`: `async fn count() Task<int>` with `return 1`. With no declared result, `async fn run()` means `Task<void>` and is fine. A fallible task, `Task<int>!`, is not a task result either; a task that yields a fallible value, `Task<int!>`, is.\n\n<!-- ubel-test: error TYPE-144 -->\n```ubel\nasync fn count() int {\n    return 1\n}\n\nfn main() {\n}\n```\n\n```text\n>>> error[TYPE-144]: an async function returns `Task<T>`, found `int`\n  --> 1:1\n    |\n  1 | async fn count() int {\n    | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n<<<\n```\n\n**Fix:** Declare the task: `async fn count() Task<int>`. The body still returns the `int`.\n")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/errors-and-optionals.md", "`async fn` and `await` are part of the design and are checked by the\ncompiler: an `async fn` is HIGH tier only\n([PARSE-004](../errors/parse.md#parse-004)), and `await` needs a `Task`\n([TYPE-106](../errors/type.md#type-106)). The runtime side is not built, so\nthere is nothing to run yet; an Async chapter will be added when there is.", "`async fn` and `await` work in every tier. An async function declares the task it returns,\n`async fn f() Task<int>`, a call builds a task that has not started, and `await` runs it. There\nare no threads yet, so a task runs where it is awaited. See [Async](./async.md).")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/project-status.md", "- Async and synchronization are designed and not built (`docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md`, decided\n  2026-11-14): tasks run in parallel on a thread pool in every tier, `async` and `await` become\n  compiler-generated state machines, a compile-time rule decides what may be shared between\n  tasks, and the usual locks, atomics and channels ship. Next: the `using` cleanup, then the\n  async core", "- Async and synchronization are designed (`docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md`, decided 2026-11-14): tasks\n  run in parallel on a thread pool in every tier, `async` and `await` become compiler-generated\n  state machines, a compile-time rule decides what may be shared between tasks, and the usual\n  locks, atomics and channels ship. The async core has landed (2026-11-17): `async fn` with a\n  `Task<T>` result, cold tasks, `await`, async blocks and every tier, all without threads. Next:\n  the `using` cleanup, then threads in the interpreter")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/project-status.md", "- `async fn`, `await` and `Task<T>` type-check but cannot run: no program can build a `Task`,\n  an `async fn` call has its declared return type, and `await` does nothing at run time", "- A task is awaited at most once, and a second `await` of the same task stops the program when it\n  runs; there is no compile-time check for it yet. An async function cannot be a trait method, and\n  the native backend does not build async code")
    patch_replace_text("web/site/src/guide/async.md", "The order of delivery starts with making `using` run its cleanup, then the\nasync core. [Project Status](../project-status.md) tracks it. An Async chapter\nwith working examples will replace this page when there is a runtime to\nexercise.", "The async core is built. Delivery continues with making `using` run its cleanup, then threads in the\ninterpreter and the sharing rule. [Project Status](../project-status.md) tracks it, and this guide\ngrows as those land.")
    patch_replace_text("crates/ubel_llvm/src/emit.rs", "    fn lambda(&mut self, e: &Expr, lam: &Lambda) -> R<Val> {", "    fn lambda(&mut self, e: &Expr, lam: &Lambda) -> R<Val> {\n        if lam.is_async_block {\n            return unsupported(e.span, \"async blocks\");\n        }")
    patch_replace_block_keep("web/site/src/guide/async.md", "# Async", "The record is `docs/ASYNC_SYNC_DESIGN.md`. In brief, the decisions are:", "\n> **Status: the async core runs.** A call to an `async fn` builds a task that has not started,\n> `await` runs it where it is awaited, and `async { ... }` makes a task from a block. All of it\n> works in every tier. There are no threads yet: a task runs inline where it is awaited, so\n> nothing here runs in parallel. Parallelism is the next stage of a larger design, summarised below.\n\n## Async functions and `await`\n\nAn async function declares the task it returns, `Task<T>`, and its body returns a `T`. A call does\nnot run the body. It builds a task, and `await` runs the task and yields the `T`. A function with\nno result is written without a return type and means `Task<void>`:\n\n<!-- ubel-test: run -->\n```ubel\nasync fn double(x: int) Task<int> {\n    println(\"running\")\n    return x * 2\n}\n\nasync fn greet() {\n    println(\"hello\")\n}\n\nasync fn main() {\n    let t = double(21)\n    println(\"built, not run\")\n    let n = await t\n    println(n)\n    await greet()\n}\n```\n\n```text\nbuilt, not run\nrunning\n42\nhello\n```\n\n`main` may be async. It is run to completion, since nothing else could start async code yet.\n\n## Blocks\n\n`async { ... }` is a task over a block. It captures what it uses from the scope around it as a\nclosure does, `return` ends the block, and it runs nothing until it is awaited:\n\n<!-- ubel-test: run -->\n```ubel\nasync fn main() {\n    let offset = 2\n    let t = async {\n        println(\"in the block\")\n        return 40 + offset\n    }\n    println(\"block built\")\n    println(await t)\n}\n```\n\n```text\nblock built\nin the block\n42\n```\n\n## Rules\n\n`await` is legal only directly inside an async function or an async block, in any tier. Anywhere\nelse, including inside a plain lambda in an async function, it is\n[TYPE-143](../errors/type.md#type-143):\n\n<!-- ubel-test: error TYPE-143 -->\n```ubel\nasync fn one() Task<int> {\n    return 1\n}\n\nfn plain() int {\n    return await one()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n}\n```\n\nThe declared result must be a task. `async fn count() int` is\n[TYPE-144](../errors/type.md#type-144):\n\n<!-- ubel-test: error TYPE-144 -->\n```ubel\nasync fn count() int {\n    return 1\n}\n\nfn main() {\n}\n```\n\n`await` needs a `Task<T>`. Awaiting an ordinary value is\n[TYPE-106](../errors/type.md#type-106):\n\n<!-- ubel-test: error TYPE-106 -->\n```ubel\nasync fn run() {\n    let n = 5\n    let m = await n\n}\n\nfn main() {\n}\n```\n\nOther rules:\n\n- A task is awaited at most once. A second `await` of the same task stops the program with an\n  error. There is no compile-time check for it yet.\n- Async methods work in an `impl` block. In a trait, or in an impl of a trait, they are\n  [PARSE-004](../errors/parse.md#parse-004) until a later stage.\n- `async`, `await` and `Task` are reserved words\n  ([Keywords](../reference/keywords-and-operators.md)).\n- The native backend does not build async code yet.\n\n## The decided design (the async core is built, threads are not)\n\n")
)
